    description: "The sixth full-length, Elite-difficulty English paper matching the real GL Assessment 11+ structure exactly: 28 reading comprehension questions on the original passage 'The Bookbinder's Apprentice', 9 spelling and 9 grammar-mistake questions in the segment 'find the mistake, or choose N' format, and 8 cloze questions. Fully re-authored on 2026-08-06 alongside english-gl-11-elite (every one of the 54 questions rewritten in place; ids, section split and total marks unchanged), for the same reason: a real marked student report showed a strong candidate scoring 83-85% on the hardest papers in this series with comprehension at or near 100%, which made this paper — written at the original Elite level — a wasted attempt. Section A now applies the no-easy-elimination rule throughout, with retrieval distractors pulled from other true details in the same passage: a 'let alone' construction whose two halves one distractor deliberately inverts, two competing true obstacles in the same paragraph where only one answers the question actually asked, and a three-item list whose closest wrong option differs by two single words. The eight inference questions are built on motivation and social reasoning rather than retrieval: why a master turns specifically to thread tension every time a question comes close, what hiding one feature of a book rather than the book itself reveals, why a funeral deadline is needed for the plot to work at all, what pressing an initial into leather 'without quite deciding to' admits, and why a failure can be avoided for years without ever being denied. Vocabulary-in-context uses six words with strong everyday-meaning traps, including the trade senses of 'quire' (with 'choir' offered as a sound-alike), 'pare' (with 'pair'), 'commission', 'tension', 'plainly' and 'true'. Sections B-D use nine named grammar-mistake subtopics and eight cloze subtopics that appear nowhere else on the platform and do not overlap english-gl-11-elite's new list: 'whom' where the pronoun is a clause subject, 'between' versus 'among', 'raise' versus 'rise', a dangling perfect participle phrase, 'didn't used to', nonstandard 'irregardless', 'who's' for 'whose', 'besides' for 'beside', and a no-mistake item on 'criteria' as a plural; and, for cloze, the fronted 'so fine a binding' intensifier order, the 'hardly... when' correlative, 'be to' for an arranged future in the past, a modal perfect inside a third conditional, proximity agreement after 'either... or', 'despite' plus a gerund against clause-taking concessives, 'whichever' for a choice within a defined set, and the fixed idiom 'had better'. Spelling moved to maintenance, conscience, argument, colleague, parallel, immediately and nuisance, plus two no-mistake items on stationery/stationary and practice/practise. Calibrated to land the same ~70% as english-gl-11-elite for a student currently scoring 83-85% on the Elite+ papers. Note for future sessions: questions eh500-eh514 are also reused by the wilsons-school-style diagnostic mock, which became harder as a side effect; ids were kept in place deliberately rather than renumbered. Not copied from any third-party paper — 100% original Summit Tuition content.",
